// Hand-curated trophy awards: who won which trophy in which competition.
//
// This file exists for exactly one purpose: the trophies whose award rule is
// classified `manual` in before-other-importers/trophies.json5. Every other
// trophy in the catalog is either recorded by the source data itself
// (`direct_source`) or computed from imported match events
// (`max_count`/`max_spp_sum`/`career_threshold`), so no human ever has to
// name its winner. A `manual` trophy has neither: no underlying statistic
// determines it, and no importer records it, so its winners can only be
// entered by hand -- here.
//
// There are currently exactly two of them:
//
//   Season MVP          Voted on by all coaches after the season; no
//                       underlying statistic determines it.
//   Gudarnas Förkämpe   The Chaos Cup winner nominates three of their players
//                       and rolls a D3 to choose one; no underlying statistic
//                       determines it.
//
// Both are player trophies, which is why an entry names a player and no team:
// a player never changes teams, so the award's team era is the winning
// player's own and the API derives it from the resolved player. Nothing has
// to state it here.
//
// The `trophyAwards` array below is deliberately EMPTY. The historical
// winners are not known yet; this file is the place to record them as they
// are established, one entry at a time. An empty array imports nothing and
// fails nothing.
//
// WHY THIS FILE LIVES IN after-other-importers
//
// An entry references a competition and a player by external id, and both
// are created by the BBL and TP importers, not by this tool. Curating them
// before those importers ran would resolve nothing (every entry would be
// skipped with an "could not resolve reference" error), so this phase --
// which runs after them -- is the only one where these references exist.
// Same reasoning as position-availability.json5 in this directory.
//
// ENTRY SHAPE
//
//   trophy       The trophy's exact curated `name` from
//                before-other-importers/trophies.json5, as a plain string --
//                NOT an external-id reference. Trophies carry no shared
//                "Name"-system id (the same name under a different
//                competition tier is a genuinely different trophy), and this
//                file is authored alongside trophies.json5 anyway, so the
//                curated name is the identity already in front of the
//                curator. Matched exactly and case-sensitively; a name no
//                trophy carries -- or one two trophies share -- skips the
//                entry with an import error.
//
//   competition  An external-id reference to the competition the trophy was
//                awarded for, in whichever source system imported it. BBL
//                competitions are keyed by the numeric id in their site link
//                (see after-other-importers/competitions.json5 for the full
//                list of which number is which season).
//
//   player       An external-id reference to the winning player, in whichever
//                source system imported them. BBL players are keyed by their
//                numeric `pid`.
//
// The trophy's own scope is still enforced server-side: awarding a trophy
// curated for one competition group in a competition belonging to another is
// rejected, so a mistyped competition id fails loudly rather than recording a
// wrong award.
//
// WORKED EXAMPLE -- the shape to copy, not live data. Delete the comment
// markers, replace the ids, and drop it into the array below:
//
//   {
//     trophy: 'Season MVP',
//     competition: { system: 'tloeg.bbleague.se', id: '10' },
//     player: { system: 'tloeg.bbleague.se', id: '388' },
//   },
//
{
  externalSystems: [
    { name: 'tloeg.bbleague.se', category: 'imported_data_source' },
    { name: 'tourplay.net', category: 'imported_data_source' },
  ],
  trophyAwards: [],
}
